# L6 Inventory Management

Flask-based inventory and datacenter management application with role-based access control, audit logging, soft delete and restore flows, and automated tests.

## Features

- Authentication with role-aware routing for admin and user views
- Session-based access control for protected pages and selected APIs
- Login rate limiting on the login route
- Inventory management APIs (create, list, update, soft delete, restore, hard delete)
- Datacenter management APIs (create, list, update, soft delete, restore, hard delete)
- Audit logging for key actions
- Admin user creation flow with temporary-password enforcement
- Pytest test suite covering login, inventory, datacenter, and security behavior

## Tech Stack

- Python 3
- Flask
- Flask-Limiter
- SQLite
- Pytest

## Project Structure

- app.py: Main Flask app, routes, auth logic, and API handlers
- db.py: Database connection and Flask app teardown integration
- schema.sql: Database schema and seed inserts
- models/: Data-access helpers for inventory and datacenter
- templates/: HTML templates for UI pages
- static/: Frontend assets (CSS and JavaScript)
- tests/: Pytest suite and test fixtures
- Dockerfile: Container image definition

## Prerequisites

- Python 3.11+ (3.12 recommended)
- pip
- Optional: Docker Desktop

## Local Setup

1. Create and activate a virtual environment.

   Windows PowerShell:

   python -m venv .venv
   .\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1

2. Install dependencies.

   pip install -r requirements.txt

3. Set environment variables.

   Windows PowerShell:

   $env:FLASK_APP = "app.py"
	$env:SECRET_KEY = "change-this-to-a-strong-random-secret"
   $env:DATABASE_PATH = "$(Get-Location)\instance\data.db"

4. Initialize the database.

   flask initdb

5. Create an admin user (interactive command).

   flask create-admin

6. Run the app.

   flask run

7. Open in browser.

   http://127.0.0.1:5000

## Docker Setup

1. Build image.

   docker build -t flask-app .

2. Run container.

   docker run -it -p 8001:5000 -e SECRET_KEY=your-strong-secret flask-app

3. Open in browser.

   http://127.0.0.1:8001

Note: If you want persistent database storage in Docker, mount a host path to a container directory and set DATABASE_PATH to that mounted location.

## Running Tests

Run full suite:

python -m pytest -q

Run a single test file:

python -m pytest tests\test_security.py -q

Run selected files:

python -m pytest tests\test_login.py tests\test_inventory.py tests\test_datacenter.py -q

## Environment Variables

- SECRET_KEY: Flask session signing key (required for secure session behavior)
- DATABASE_PATH: SQLite database file path
- FLASK_APP: Flask entrypoint (app.py)

## Key Routes

Pages:

- /login
- /logout
- /change-password
- /admin
- /user
- /inventory
- /datacenter
- /actionsAudit
- /admin/create-user

Inventory API:

- POST /api/item
- GET /api/items
- PUT /api/item or /api/item/<id>
- DELETE /api/item/<id>
- POST /api/item/restore/<id>
- GET /api/items/deleted
- DELETE /api/item/hard-delete/<id>

Datacenter API:

- POST /api/datacenter
- GET /api/datacenters
- PUT /api/datacenter/<id>
- DELETE /api/datacenter/<id>
- POST /api/datacenter/restore/<id>
- GET /api/datacenters/deleted
- DELETE /api/datacenter/hard-delete/<id>

Audit API:

- GET /api/actionsAudit

## Security Notes

- Session cookies are configured as HttpOnly, Secure, and SameSite=Lax
- Login endpoint is rate-limited
- Admin-only endpoints are protected by role checks

## Troubleshooting

- Session errors during tests usually indicate missing SECRET_KEY configuration
- If port 5000 is in use, run with a different port:

  flask run --port 5001

- If SQLite path errors occur, ensure DATABASE_PATH points to a writable location
